# Line endings: LF in the repository, native in the working tree.
#
# Windows checkouts still get CRLF working copies (core.autocrlf=true handles
# that); this only pins what git STORES. Until now the convention held by habit
# alone, which made it a trap: style.css happened to be stored with CRLF, so
# editing it through a tool that writes LF - then a normal `git add`, which
# normalizes - rewrote all 3800 lines and produced a whole-file diff for a
# 78-line change (2026-07-25). With `text=auto` the stored form no longer
# depends on which editor or script touched the file last.
* text=auto

# Never transform these bytes. Binary data can contain 0x0D0A sequences that mean
# nothing about lines, and "normalizing" them would corrupt the file. `text=auto`
# above only guesses from a NUL byte in the first 8000 bytes, which a small icon
# or a short font can lack entirely - so the formats that could plausibly land
# here are named rather than left to the heuristic.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.db binary

# Vendored third-party builds are stored byte-for-byte as published, so the
# sha256 recorded in NOTICE stays checkable against the upstream release asset
# by anyone who wants to verify what this tree ships. `text=auto` above would
# not in fact touch the current file (htmx.min.js is one line with no newline
# at all), but that is a property of this particular build, not a guarantee
# about the next one - so the rule is stated rather than relied on.
static/js/vendor/** -text

# Shell scripts must keep LF whatever the checkout platform does. Dockerfile does
# `COPY . .` from the build context, so a CRLF script authored on Windows reaches
# a Linux image with \r in its shebang and fails with "no such file or directory"
# - naming the interpreter it can't find, which reads like anything but a
# line-ending problem.
*.sh text eol=lf
